LiquidGlassWinUI 1.0.3
Upstream: https://github.com/luckyelysia/LiquidGlassWinUI
License: MIT (see LICENSE). CustomEffectRuntimeNative.dll is the unmodified x64
binary from the pinned LiquidGlassWinUI 1.0.3 package.

Local adaptations retained from the supplied UI reference:
- BindScalarExpression/StopScalarExpression let the host couple RefThickness
  to compositor-owned capsule geometry and inflation without UI timers.
- ConnectToVisual/DisconnectFromVisual leases reuse the same pooled glass
  pipeline for a dynamically sized Composition sprite in the fluid-tab demo.
- Unused upstream DebugEffect stub removed; its Debug.hlsl is not shipped or used.
- .NET 10 only, Windows App SDK 2.2.0, Win2D 1.3.2; optional AI/ML/widgets excluded.
- Current XamlRoot rasterization scale reaches the shader Dpr slot (124).
- ShapeInset uses the existing ShadowFactor slot (76); host sets zero so tint
  and highlights cover the entire shape without an untinted outer gutter.
- HLSL edge antialiasing spans one output pixel instead of a broad exterior blur.
- FillWindowBackground uses the unused slot 80, keeping the 128-byte native ABI.
  Optional opaque tint backing covers the optical shape's AA/exterior. The host
  now disables it to cut tint outside the highlight. A full-window glass client
  shares coordinates with the HWND and leaves final rounding to DWM, without a
  GDI window region. Control/capsule transparency is unchanged.
- Tint concentrations directly color transmission, Fresnel and highlights.
- Magnification remains 1.0 on every glass surface, including enlarged tabs.
- Optical displacement/dispersion are normalized in control coordinates then
  scaled by contentUvSize before atlas sampling, preventing frame-to-frame jitter.
- Host supplies a visible local captured layer under the original backdrop brush;
  Gaussian runs on the full monitor before cropping. Cards apply no extra blur.
- HLSL regression tests render synthetic atlases through WARP, without capture.

- All surfaces use analytic curved normals and outward inverse UV lookup,
  moving the underlying image toward the glass center. This contracts and bends
  track edges without whole-image scaling. Ray travel is smoothly bounded by
  optical depth: reach(d)=0.75*d*d/(d+0.20*RefThickness*Dpr). Its onset has zero
  slope and its growth stays below 0.75; a harmonic Snell bound avoids local
  inverse-map folds and the resulting sharp notches at track/glass junctions.
  The footprint follows the same ray mapping. There is no inflation-only optical
  mode or separate capsule pipeline; slot 84 is unused again. WARP checks inward
  image flow at identity zoom, curved ends, DPI and atlas invariance. Ray travel
  scales once through Dpr, preserving logical refraction strength across monitors.
- Rays and their sampling footprint smoothly return to identity at the optical
  contour. Interior curved refraction remains; exterior track pixels join the
  interior without a positional jump. WARP follows connected RGB strokes across
  both rounded edges, including one-physical-pixel lines at 100-300% DPI.
  Rendered coordinate fields also check positive Jacobians at rest and enlarged
  sizes; connected strokes alone cannot detect folded/sharply notched sampling.

The native material ABI remains unchanged. Test source:
../../Tests/YogaITS.ShaderChecks; no obsolete wallpaper or WinUI 2 probe is required.

- Snell displacement uses the algebraically equivalent tangent-difference form
  with sin/cos components, avoiding expensive asin/sin/tan expansions. This keeps
  the contour-continuity fix within the native compositor library's instruction
  limit. Every glass-enabled build compiles lib_4_0_level_9_3_ps_only with flags 0x8800 before
  replacing output; standalone pixel-shader tests are additional validation.
